WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.500
The first film showed how rhythms and cues time responses.

2
00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.140
What happens when cue patterns change for years?

3
00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:09.660
Return to the same dial.

4
00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:13.060
The nearest dial still holds a cherry bud.

5
00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:16.540
This time, stack many years behind it.

6
00:00:19.883 --> 00:00:23.763
Long-term change can come from natural events or from people.

7
00:00:25.363 --> 00:00:27.323
Wildfires and floods can happen naturally.

8
00:00:28.683 --> 00:00:31.523
People can cause pollution and habitat destruction.

9
00:00:32.983 --> 00:00:37.503
Change can alter population size, location, relationships, or timing.

10
00:00:38.903 --> 00:00:42.103
Here we will follow timing, using one group of cherry trees.

11
00:00:43.217 --> 00:00:47.577
Suppose winter and early spring become warmer across many years.

12
00:00:48.877 --> 00:00:51.457
A tree that responds to warmth may bloom earlier.

13
00:00:52.757 --> 00:00:56.317
On each new dial, the red warmth band reaches the bud sooner.

14
00:00:57.617 --> 00:01:01.057
The green bloom marker slides earlier on this schematic dial.

15
00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.100
But warmth is not the only cue.

16
00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:07.220
Many buds need enough cold first.

17
00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:12.520
If winter becomes too warm, some may open late or unevenly.

18
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.060
Warming does not force every response earlier.

19
00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.980
Different timing systems can give different results.

20
00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:25.770
Now predict. One spring has an unusually early bloom.

21
00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:29.970
Does that single year prove the long-term pattern moved?

22
00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:32.950
The rest of the record stays hidden.

23
00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:38.190
Hold your answer while one green point appears on an empty timeline.

24
00:01:44.283 --> 00:01:48.243
No. One year can differ for many reasons.

25
00:01:49.683 --> 00:01:54.223
A long-term claim needs repeated observations made with the same rule.

26
00:01:55.683 --> 00:01:57.743
Observers use a fixed rule for peak bloom.

27
00:01:58.963 --> 00:02:03.583
At the Tidal Basin, observers recorded peak bloom across nearly a century.

28
00:02:04.767 --> 00:02:06.587
The record uses day of year.

29
00:02:07.787 --> 00:02:10.307
A smaller day number means an earlier date.

30
00:02:11.527 --> 00:02:13.727
That direction can feel backward at first.

31
00:02:15.047 --> 00:02:18.107
Imagine the year as a ruler from winter toward summer.

32
00:02:19.427 --> 00:02:21.167
Moving left means blooming earlier.

33
00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:25.680
Scientists can group years and compare mean bloom days.

34
00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:28.200
The plotted means wiggle.

35
00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.580
The line never becomes a smooth staircase.

36
00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:35.300
Several groups are later than the group before.

37
00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:41.040
After the group with the latest mean, the means move earlier, though not in a steady

38
00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:42.040
line.

39
00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:44.270
Compare two groups on screen.

40
00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:47.850
Subtract the smaller mean from the larger mean.

41
00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:51.210
The smaller mean marks the earlier bloom.

42
00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:55.110
The difference shown is evidence of shifted timing.

43
00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:57.770
Be careful with the claim.

44
00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:02.450
These trees in one city do not represent every species.

45
00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:07.530
The dates show a pattern, but they alone cannot prove what caused it.

46
00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:10.970
Testing cause needs temperature evidence too.

47
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.660
The dial, record, and limit fit together.

48
00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:17.800
Organisms use timing systems.

49
00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.980
Long-term conditions can move the cues they meet.

50
00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:27.060
Responses may shift, while the details still depend on the organism.

51
00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:30.740
A careful claim keeps both pattern and limit.
